1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,246
Nobody knows for sure
what this painting means.

2
00:00:04,246 --> 00:00:06,066
Not the kings who owned it.

3
00:00:06,086 --> 00:00:07,986
Not the monks who guarded it.

4
00:00:08,006 --> 00:00:10,612
Not the scholars who have
spent their lives on it.

5
00:00:11,352 --> 00:00:16,838
The man who painted it left no
letters, no diary, not even a title.

6
00:00:17,708 --> 00:00:21,288
For five hundred years, people
have looked into this garden

7
00:00:21,308 --> 00:00:23,848
and seen completely different things.

8
00:00:23,868 --> 00:00:25,048
A party.

9
00:00:25,068 --> 00:00:26,168
A sermon.

10
00:00:26,188 --> 00:00:27,448
A heresy.

11
00:00:27,468 --> 00:00:28,718
A song.

12
00:00:29,436 --> 00:00:32,616
This is the story of The
Garden of Earthly Delights,

13
00:00:32,636 --> 00:00:35,057
and of everyone who tried to read it.

14
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:41,995
He was born around 1450, in a
Dutch town called 's-Hertogenbosch.

15
00:00:42,517 --> 00:00:45,220
His name was Jheronimus van Aken.

16
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:47,970
His grandfather was a painter.

17
00:00:47,990 --> 00:00:49,410
So was his father.

18
00:00:49,430 --> 00:00:51,250
So were most of his uncles.

19
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:53,363
Painting was the family trade.

20
00:00:53,973 --> 00:00:59,953
Around 1486, he joined the Brotherhood of
Our Lady, a devout religious brotherhood

21
00:00:59,973 --> 00:01:02,099
of the town's leading citizens.

22
00:01:02,709 --> 00:01:06,486
He signed a few of his paintings
with the name of his town.

23
00:01:06,506 --> 00:01:08,289
Jheronimus Bosch.

24
00:01:08,309 --> 00:01:10,012
He never dated them.

25
00:01:10,622 --> 00:01:13,002
And that is almost all we know.

26
00:01:13,022 --> 00:01:14,362
No letters.

27
00:01:14,382 --> 00:01:15,722
No diary.

28
00:01:15,742 --> 00:01:18,122
Not one word about his own work.

29
00:01:18,142 --> 00:01:22,584
Just a few lines in the town records,
and in the brotherhood's account books.

30
00:01:23,324 --> 00:01:26,984
About twenty-five paintings
are confidently his today.

31
00:01:27,004 --> 00:01:29,187
This is the most famous of them.

32
00:01:31,087 --> 00:01:32,667
It is a triptych.

33
00:01:32,687 --> 00:01:35,867
Three panels of oak,
almost two metres tall,

34
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:38,475
more than three metres
wide when it's open.

35
00:01:39,325 --> 00:01:42,105
Closed, it doesn't look strange at all.

36
00:01:42,125 --> 00:01:43,625
A grey world.

37
00:01:43,645 --> 00:01:46,585
Water, land, plants.

38
00:01:46,605 --> 00:01:48,265
No animals yet.

39
00:01:48,285 --> 00:01:49,535
No people.

40
00:01:49,902 --> 00:01:52,282
It is the third day of Creation.

41
00:01:52,302 --> 00:01:56,922
Up in the corner sits God,
tiny, with a book on his lap.

42
00:01:56,942 --> 00:01:59,962
Across the top, two lines from the Psalms.

43
00:01:59,982 --> 00:02:02,122
For he spake, and it was done.

44
00:02:02,142 --> 00:02:04,653
For he commanded, and they were created.

45
00:02:05,523 --> 00:02:07,227
Then the doors open.

46
00:02:08,487 --> 00:02:10,707
On the left, Eden.

47
00:02:10,727 --> 00:02:12,787
God presents Eve to Adam.

48
00:02:12,807 --> 00:02:17,667
The museum says Bosch first painted a
different scene here, the creation of Eve,

49
00:02:17,687 --> 00:02:19,416
and then changed his mind.

50
00:02:20,026 --> 00:02:22,246
On the right, Hell.

51
00:02:22,266 --> 00:02:27,102
Fire, darkness, torture, and
enormous musical instruments.

52
00:02:27,712 --> 00:02:32,412
A monster with the head of a bird
sits on a throne, eating the damned,

53
00:02:32,432 --> 00:02:34,652
and passing them out below.

54
00:02:34,672 --> 00:02:40,222
A pig in a nun's veil leans on a man,
trying to get him to sign a document.

55
00:02:40,352 --> 00:02:44,652
And in the middle of it all, a man
made of tree trunks and a broken shell,

56
00:02:44,672 --> 00:02:46,732
with a tavern inside him.

57
00:02:46,752 --> 00:02:50,382
One historian thought that
face might be Bosch himself.

58
00:02:51,252 --> 00:02:53,952
And in the middle, the garden.

59
00:02:53,972 --> 00:02:55,952
Crowds of naked people.

60
00:02:55,972 --> 00:02:57,312
Giant birds.

61
00:02:57,332 --> 00:02:58,592
Giant fruit.

62
00:02:58,612 --> 00:03:00,672
Riders circling a pool.

63
00:03:00,692 --> 00:03:03,207
Strange buildings standing in the water.

64
00:03:03,817 --> 00:03:05,397
Is it paradise?

65
00:03:05,417 --> 00:03:07,237
Or is it a warning?

66
00:03:07,257 --> 00:03:09,977
People have been arguing
about it ever since.

67
00:03:11,877 --> 00:03:14,387
Bosch died in August 1516.

68
00:03:14,597 --> 00:03:19,185
The first time anyone writes about
this painting is the very next year.

69
00:03:19,795 --> 00:03:25,695
In 1517, Antonio de Beatis,
secretary to an Italian cardinal,

70
00:03:25,715 --> 00:03:28,335
is travelling through
Europe with his master.

71
00:03:28,355 --> 00:03:33,151
In Brussels, they visit the palace
of Count Henry the Third of Nassau.

72
00:03:33,761 --> 00:03:36,221
In his journal, de Beatis notes

73
00:03:36,241 --> 00:03:40,243
that there are some panels on which
bizarre things have been painted.

74
00:03:40,983 --> 00:03:44,243
In the same palace, he
notices something else.

75
00:03:44,263 --> 00:03:45,843
An enormous bed.

76
00:03:45,863 --> 00:03:50,883
He writes that the Count had it made
because he liked to hold frequent banquets

77
00:03:50,903 --> 00:03:52,959
and to see his guests get drunk.

78
00:03:53,699 --> 00:03:55,759
So what was the painting for?

79
00:03:55,779 --> 00:03:58,799
Some historians think it
was made for a wedding.

80
00:03:58,819 --> 00:04:03,039
Others think that famous bed was
really a ceremonial platform,

81
00:04:03,059 --> 00:04:06,479
that the painting hung in a hall
for councils and court hearings,

82
00:04:06,499 --> 00:04:08,493
as a lesson about justice.

83
00:04:09,103 --> 00:04:11,373
Even the furniture is disputed.

84
00:04:11,853 --> 00:04:17,113
The Prado believes the triptych was ordered
by Henry's uncle, Engelbert of Nassau,

85
00:04:17,133 --> 00:04:20,969
who died in 1504 and named
his nephew as his heir.

86
00:04:22,869 --> 00:04:29,258
By the 1560s, it belongs to Henry's
nephew, a prince, William of Orange.

87
00:04:29,868 --> 00:04:32,968
The Netherlands are ruled
by the King of Spain.

88
00:04:32,988 --> 00:04:36,488
And William becomes the leader
of the revolt against him.

89
00:04:37,098 --> 00:04:41,718
In 1568, the king's
general, the Duke of Alba,

90
00:04:41,738 --> 00:04:44,438
seizes William's property in Brussels.

91
00:04:44,458 --> 00:04:46,275
The Garden goes with it.

92
00:04:47,015 --> 00:04:51,555
The Duke gives it to his son
Fernando, born outside his marriage.

93
00:04:51,575 --> 00:04:54,043
It goes south, to Spain.

94
00:04:54,653 --> 00:04:57,673
When Fernando dies, his things are sold.

95
00:04:57,693 --> 00:05:01,833
And in 1591, the buyer
is the king himself.

96
00:05:01,853 --> 00:05:03,775
Philip the Second of Spain.

97
00:05:05,675 --> 00:05:08,455
Philip is the most
Catholic king in Europe.

98
00:05:08,475 --> 00:05:12,202
And he collects Bosch's
paintings, one after another.

99
00:05:12,812 --> 00:05:18,952
In 1593, he sends the Garden to his
new monastery-palace, El Escorial,

100
00:05:18,972 --> 00:05:21,235
in the mountains outside Madrid.

101
00:05:21,845 --> 00:05:26,465
The record of the gift describes a
painting of the variety of the world.

102
00:05:26,485 --> 00:05:28,865
And it gives the name people knew it by.

103
00:05:28,885 --> 00:05:30,526
The strawberry tree.

104
00:05:31,266 --> 00:05:33,966
But by now, some Spaniards are whispering

105
00:05:33,986 --> 00:05:37,004
that Bosch's pictures
are tainted with heresy.

106
00:05:37,614 --> 00:05:43,034
In 1605, a monk of the
Escorial, Fray José de Sigüenza,

107
00:05:43,054 --> 00:05:44,645
writes their defence.

108
00:05:45,125 --> 00:05:49,555
The garden, he says, is
about the vanity of pleasure.

109
00:05:49,605 --> 00:05:54,109
Sweet, like the taste of a
strawberry, and gone just as fast.

110
00:05:54,849 --> 00:05:58,109
And he writes the line people
still quote about Bosch.

111
00:05:58,129 --> 00:06:02,589
Other painters, he says, show
people as they look on the outside.

112
00:06:02,609 --> 00:06:05,875
Bosch dares to paint
them as they are inside.

113
00:06:06,745 --> 00:06:10,753
The Garden stays at the Escorial
for more than three hundred years.

114
00:06:12,653 --> 00:06:18,153
Then, in 1947, a German
scholar, Wilhelm Fraenger,

115
00:06:18,173 --> 00:06:20,212
turns everything upside down.

116
00:06:20,822 --> 00:06:24,642
The garden, he says,
isn't a warning at all.

117
00:06:24,662 --> 00:06:28,332
It's a joyful new paradise,
a world of innocence.

118
00:06:28,942 --> 00:06:34,012
He claims Bosch secretly belonged
to a sect of heretics, the Adamites,

119
00:06:34,062 --> 00:06:37,043
and that the painting was
made for their grand master.

120
00:06:37,653 --> 00:06:39,233
The idea caught on.

121
00:06:39,253 --> 00:06:41,311
Popular books still repeat it.

122
00:06:41,921 --> 00:06:44,461
But most scholars have
turned away from it.

123
00:06:44,481 --> 00:06:48,701
Bosch was a member of a Catholic
brotherhood in good standing.

124
00:06:48,721 --> 00:06:52,394
And his greatest collector was
the king who hunted heretics.

125
00:06:53,134 --> 00:06:57,434
Today, the museum reads the
middle panel as a false paradise,

126
00:06:57,454 --> 00:06:59,914
given over to the sin of lust.

127
00:06:59,934 --> 00:07:03,871
Between the first sin on the left,
and its punishment on the right.

128
00:07:05,771 --> 00:07:10,582
And then, in February 2014,
the painting goes viral.

129
00:07:11,322 --> 00:07:15,542
A twenty-year-old music student
in Oklahoma, Amelia Hamrick,

130
00:07:15,562 --> 00:07:18,146
is looking at the Hell
panel late one night.

131
00:07:18,626 --> 00:07:22,766
Down in the corner, a sinner
lies pinned under a giant lute.

132
00:07:22,786 --> 00:07:25,806
And across his backside, there are notes.

133
00:07:25,826 --> 00:07:27,537
Painted on a stave.

134
00:07:28,277 --> 00:07:33,857
She turns them into modern notation,
records them, and posts the tune online.

135
00:07:33,877 --> 00:07:38,280
The internet calls it the
500-year-old butt song from Hell.

136
00:07:39,150 --> 00:07:42,330
But music historians
point out a few problems.

137
00:07:42,350 --> 00:07:44,010
She wasn't the first.

138
00:07:44,030 --> 00:07:50,140
Scholars had written it out back in
1961, and a band had recorded it in 1978.

139
00:07:51,116 --> 00:07:52,616
And there's no clef.

140
00:07:52,636 --> 00:07:56,376
Without one, nobody can
say which notes those are.

141
00:07:56,396 --> 00:08:00,936
Bosch painted the same kind of
make-believe music in other works too.

142
00:08:00,956 --> 00:08:02,936
It has the look of music.

143
00:08:02,956 --> 00:08:04,206
Not the sound.

144
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:06,980
Even Hamrick had her doubts.

145
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,620
She told a reporter: I think Bosch may
have just been slapping notes up there

146
00:08:11,640 --> 00:08:13,520
to make it look like music.

147
00:08:15,420 --> 00:08:16,600
A party.

148
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:17,720
A wedding.

149
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:18,840
A sermon.

150
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:20,120
A heresy.

151
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:21,530
A song.

152
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:26,408
Five hundred years of readers, and every
one of them found something different.

153
00:08:27,148 --> 00:08:31,768
And in the middle of the garden, two
owls stare straight out of the painting.

154
00:08:31,788 --> 00:08:33,038
At you.

155
00:08:33,859 --> 00:08:36,525
It hangs in the Prado, in Madrid.
